.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm xtc_stats
is the runtime telemetry layer.  Three metric kinds:
.Bl -tag -width "histogram" -offset indent
.It counter
Monotonic non-negative count
.Po requests served, errors observed Pc .
.It gauge
Current value, can move up or down
.Po active connections, queue depth Pc .
.It histogram
Distribution; supports quantile queries
.Pq p50, p99, p999
and keeps the exact maximum.
For latency tracking.
.It dist
Online mergeable mean and population variance of a stream of
.Vt double
samples.
For a running mean/variance where the full quantile
machinery of a histogram is not needed.
.El
.Pp
.Sy Concurrency safety :
.Bl -bullet -compact -offset indent
.It
Counters are
.Em per-CPU sharded :
.Fn xtc_counter_inc
hits one cache-line-isolated atomic on the calling CPU.  No
cross-CPU coherence traffic on the hot path.  Reads sum all
shards.
.It
Gauges are a single
.Vt _Atomic int64_t .
For low-frequency-update gauges this is fine; high-frequency
gauges should be modeled as a counter pair instead.
.It
Histograms are per-CPU sharded
.Pq one log-linear bucket array per CPU ;
.Fn xtc_hist_record
hits one shard's bucket counter.
.Fn xtc_hist_quantile
merges shards under no lock
.Pq relaxed atomic loads .
The buckets are log-linear: 40 powers of two from 1 ns to
2^40 ns
.Pq about 18 minutes ,
16 sub-buckets each
.Pq 640 buckets, 5 KiB per CPU shard .
Values below 16 are exact; a larger value's bucket is at most 1/16 of
the value wide.
Each shard also keeps the exact largest value it recorded.
A record is one atomic add to its bucket and one relaxed load of the
shard's maximum
.Pq a compare-and-swap only for a new maximum ;
the count is the sum of the buckets, computed on read.
.Fn xtc_hist_quantile
returns the nearest-rank quantile: the smallest recorded value
.Fa v
with at least ceil(q * count) values at or below it, reported as the
.Em upper
bound of
.Fa v Ns 's
bucket
.Pq HdrHistogram's convention
and never more than the maximum.
So a quantile is never below the true value and at most 1/16 above it.
.Fa q
is clamped to [0, 1];
.Fa q
= 1 returns the maximum.
Values of 2^40 ns and more are counted in the top bucket, whose
quantile is the maximum.
A value of 0 or less is counted in the lowest bucket and does not
raise the maximum.
.Fn xtc_hist_max
returns the largest value recorded
.Pq 0 for an empty histogram ,
and
.Fn xtc_hist_count
the number of values.
In 1.59 and earlier the top bucket was 2^24 ns
.Pq 16.8 ms ,
every larger value was counted there, a quantile was the bucket's
.Em lower
bound, and there was no maximum: a 500 ms stall read as about 16 ms.
.It
Dists are per-CPU sharded.
Each shard holds a Welford triple
.Pq count, mean, M2 ;
.Fn xtc_dist_record
does Welford's single-pass update on the calling CPU's shard.
The Welford update is a read-modify-write of three fields, not a
single atomic add, and two threads can migrate onto the same
shard, so each shard carries a brief per-shard spinlock held only
for the handful of arithmetic ops:
.Fn xtc_dist_record
is therefore
.Em not
lock-free.
It reads no clock and allocates nothing.
.Fn xtc_dist_mean ,
.Fn xtc_dist_variance ,
.Fn xtc_dist_count ,
and
.Fn xtc_dist_stddev
snapshot every shard under its lock and merge the triples with
Chan's parallel formula
.Po
M2 = M2a + M2b + delta^2 * na*nb/(na+nb)
.Pc .
The reported variance is the
.Em population
variance
.Pq M2 / count ;
it is 0.0 for a count below 2, and
.Fn xtc_dist_mean
is 0.0 for an empty dist.
.Nm xtc_dist
is consumer-facing telemetry and is
.Em not
simulation-reachable.
.El
.Pp
.Sy Observability discipline :
the implementation is designed not to obscure observability tools.
Updating a counter compiles to one CPU-shard
.Vt sched_getcpu
.Pq cached in TLS
plus one
.Vt atomic_fetch_add ;
under
.Xr perf-stat 1
this shows up as the program's CPU pattern, not as overhead in
the telemetry layer.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_counter_create ,
.Fn xtc_gauge_create ,
.Fn xtc_hist_create
register the metric in a global registry keyed by
.Fa name
.Pq up to 64 chars .
The matching
.Fn xtc_counter_destroy ,
.Fn xtc_gauge_destroy ,
and
.Fn xtc_hist_destroy
unregister and free a metric.
The
.Fn xtc_dist_create
and
.Fn xtc_dist_destroy
pair register and release a dist the same way.
The registry supports walking via
.Fn xtc_metrics_iterate
or dumping in Prometheus exposition format via
.Fn xtc_metrics_dump_prometheus
to a file descriptor.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
xtc_counter_t *requests;
xtc_hist_t    *latency;

xtc_counter_create("rexis.requests", &requests);
xtc_hist_create  ("rexis.req_latency_ns", &latency);

/* Hot path: */
int64_t t0 = now_ns();
handle_request();
xtc_counter_inc(requests);
xtc_hist_record(latency, now_ns() - t0);

/* Periodic dump (e.g. via xtc_timer): */
xtc_metrics_dump_prometheus(scrape_fd);
.Ed
.Sh PROMETHEUS OUTPUT
.Fn xtc_metrics_dump_prometheus
emits, for each metric:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# TYPE rexis.requests counter
rexis.requests 12345
# TYPE rexis.queue_depth gauge
rexis.queue_depth 42
# TYPE rexis.req_latency_ns histogram
rexis.req_latency_ns_count 12345
rexis.req_latency_ns_p50 1500
rexis.req_latency_ns_p99 9800
rexis.req_latency_ns_p999 24000
rexis.req_latency_ns_max 183167000
# TYPE rexis.batch_size summary
rexis.batch_size_count 12345
rexis.batch_size_mean 37.5
rexis.batch_size_variance 12.25
rexis.batch_size_stddev 3.5
.Ed
.Pp
A trivial scrape server reads
.Fa fd
and forwards to HTTP.  See
.Pa examples/05_rexis/metrics.c
for an example dump-on-timer pattern.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_tuning_check
runs libxtc's host-tuning advisor: cheap, read-only probes (CPU
governor, intel_pstate, transparent hugepages, vm swappiness, sched
autogroup, io_uring-under-seccomp) that each log one
.Dv XTC_LOG_INFO
line to the
.Xr xtc_log 3
default sink when the host is not in the recommended state for a
low-latency runtime.  It is silent on a well-tuned host, Linux-only (a
no-op elsewhere), and never writes
.Pa /proc
or
.Pa /sys .
This is the one diagnostic libxtc itself emits through
.Xr xtc_log 3 :
.Xr xtc_app_start 3
calls it by default, but a consumer that brings carriers up directly
via
.Xr xtc_exec 3
should call
.Fn xtc_tuning_check
once at startup, after installing its
.Fn xtc_log_set_default
sink, to receive the advisories.  Otherwise
.Xr xtc_log 3
is application-fill -- libxtc streams no other internal log lines --
and
.Nm xtc_metrics
is likewise a registry the application populates; libxtc registers no
internal metrics (use
.Xr xtc_exec 3 Ns 's
.Fn xtc_exec_loop_stats
for per-carrier internals).
